<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · region</p><h1 class="enc-title">Nagorno-Karabakh</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Nagorno-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a> is a mountainous region in the south-east of the Lesser <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/caucasus">Caucasus</a>, within <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, historically with an Armenian majority. Made an autonomous oblast of Soviet Azerbaijan in 1923, it became the focus of the Karabakh movement and of a war in which Armenian forces took control by 1994. Azerbaijan regained much of the surrounding area in 2020 and the rest in September 2023, after which almost all its <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> fled.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/nagorno-karabakh.jpg" alt="Nagorno-Karabakh" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Aivazovsky · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Location_Nagorno-Karabakh2.png" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Nagorno-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a> is a region of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> on the south-eastern stretch of the Lesser Caucasus. It forms part of the larger region of Karabakh, which lies between the rivers Kura and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Araxes</a>, and spans the area between Lower Karabakh and Syunik. Its terrain consists mostly of mountains and forest. The region is usually identified with the former <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nagorno-karabakh-autonomous-oblast">Nagorno-Karabakh Autonomous Oblast</a>, which covered 4,400 square kilometres, although its historical extent is about 8,223 square kilometres. Urartian inscriptions of the 9th to 7th centuries BCE name it Urtekhini, and ancient Greek sources called it Orkhistene.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>The region lies within the lands of the Kura-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aras-river">Araxes</a> culture. Its ancient population was made up of local and migrant tribes, mostly non-Indo-European. According to the prevailing western theory they intermarried with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenians">Armenians</a> who arrived after its inclusion in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> in the 2nd century BCE, or possibly the 4th; other scholars place Armenian settlement as early as the 7th century BCE. Arabs conquered the region in the mid 7th century. According to some sources the Armenian prince Sahl Smbatian revolted in 821 and founded the House of Khachen, which ruled Artsakh as a principality until the early 19th century. By 1000 the House of Khachen had proclaimed the Kingdom of Artsakh.</p><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/karabakh">Karabakh</a> became a Russian protectorate in 1805 by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-kurekchay">Treaty of Kurekchay</a>, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/persia">Persia</a> ceded it to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/russia">Russia</a> by the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-gulistan">Treaty of Gulistan</a> in 1813. In 1823 the five districts corresponding roughly to the present region were 90.8% Armenian. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/october-revolution">October Revolution</a> it passed into the Transcaucasian Democratic Federative Republic, which soon split into separate states, and from 1918 to 1920 <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a> fought a series of short wars over several regions, including this one.</p><p>On 13 February 1988 Karabakh Armenians began demonstrating in Stepanakert for unification with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/armenia">Armenia</a>, and on 20 February the regional Soviet voted 110 to 17 to ask for the transfer. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/moscow">Moscow</a> rejected the demand. Most of the region was governed by ethnic Armenians under the breakaway Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artsakh-historic-province">Artsakh</a> from the end of the first Nagorno-Karabakh War in 1994, and talks mediated by the OSCE Minsk Group did not settle its status.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The region lies within the borders of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan</a>, on average about 1,100 metres above sea level, with steppe on the Kura lowland, forests of oak, hornbeam and beech on the lower slopes, and alpine meadows higher up. The Republic of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/artsakh-historic-province">Artsakh</a>, which governed most of it from 1994, was formally dissolved on 1 January 2024.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Nagorno-Karabakh” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>The republic's third decade turned on the loss of <b>Nagorno-Karabakh</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenia">Armenia · Into the Present</a></li><li><blockquote>The question of <b>Nagorno-Karabakh</b>, given to Azerbaijan in 1921, never went away: its Armenians appealed to Khrushchev in 1962, Anton Kochinyan asked Moscow in vain for unification in 1966, and the petitions went on through the 1970s.</blockquote><a href="/empire/armenian-ssr">Armenian SSR · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li><li><blockquote>Under glasnost the dispute over <b>Nagorno-Karabakh</b>, an autonomous region with an Armenian majority, grew into open conflict and calls for independence, culminating in the Black January events in Baku.</blockquote><a href="/empire/azerbaijan">Azerbaijan · Foundation</a></li></ul></section>
